  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>&#8861;</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;values for the redox couple (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>) shows that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Co</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;ions are the strongest oxidising agents in aqueous solution.</p><p>The ions&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ti</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&nbsp;are strong reducing agents and will liberate hydrogen from a dilute acid.</p><p>So, the Correct answer is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>Cr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>.</p>",
